[Repertoire "white-italian"]
[Side "white"]
[Title "The Italian"]
[Tagline "Slow, sneaky, and very hard to blunder in. Perfect for you."]
[Chapter "Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[Plans "Now the knight takes the scenic route: Nbd2-f1-g3, where it eyes f5 and h5. Then pick your moment for d3-d4 to open the center, or go Be3 and Nh4-f5 if Black's king looks lonely. When ...Na5 or ...b5 shows up, tuck the bishop back to a2 and keep it. No rush: this structure doesn't crack, so let Black run out of useful moves first."]

{The "very quiet game". You build a fortress with c3 and d3, castle, and only then think about attacking. No gambits, no 12-move theory, no excuses.}
1. e4 {Center pawn, opens the bishop and queen. You know this one.} e5 2. Nf3 {Attack e5 and develop. Two birds, one knight.} Nc6 3. Bc4 {The Italian bishop. It stares at f7, the weakest square in Black's camp. [%cal Gc4f7]} Bc5 {Black copies you. Rude, but common. [%cal Rc5f2]}
4. c3 {Prepares d4 one day, and gives the bishop a retreat square on c2 later. [%cal Gd2d4] [%csl Gc2]} Nf6
( 4... d6 5. d3 Nf6 6. O-O {Same setup. Every road leads to c3, d3, castle.} )
( 4... Qe7 5. d3 Nf6 6. O-O d6 7. h3 {Same plan. The queen on e7 changes nothing for you.} )
5. d3 {Solid as a rock. e4 is protected forever, and you can stop worrying about it. [%csl Ge4]} d6
( 5... O-O 6. O-O d6 7. h3 {h3 stops ...Bg4 pinning your knight. Remember this move, it's in every line.} )
( 5... a6 6. O-O d6 7. h3 O-O 8. a4 Ba7 9. Re1 {Back in the main line.} )
6. O-O {King safety first. Always. Yes, even when you're bored.} O-O
( 6... a6 7. h3 O-O 8. a4 Ba7 9. Re1 )
( 6... h6 7. h3 O-O 8. Re1 {Black wasted a move. You didn't. Smile.} )
( 6... Bg4 {[%cal Rg4d1]} 7. h3 Bh5 8. Re1 {Ask the bishop a question. Don't rush g4, your king lives there.} )
7. h3 {No ...Bg4 for Black, ever. Luft for your king too, so no back-rank mate surprises. [%csl Rg4,Gh2]} a6 8. a4 {Stops ...b5 from kicking your bishop. [%csl Rb5]} Ba7 9. Re1 {Rook behind e4. Plan from here: Nbd2-f1-g3, then maybe d4. Patience wins this one. [%cal Gb1d2,Gd2f1,Gf1g3]}

[Chapter "Two Knights (3...Nf6)"]
[Plans "It's a Pianissimo now, so the same recipe: Nbd2-f1-g3, a4 against ...b5, h3 against ...Bg4. If Black plays ...d5 early, get a rook to e1 and hit e5 while their king is still in the middle. The d3-d4 break is your trump card; play it when your pieces are all out, not before."]

{Black attacks e4 straight away. The famous reply is 4.Ng5, the Fried Liver, but it leads to chaos you don't want yet. We play 4.d3: calm, solid, same plans as the Pianissimo.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 {e4 is defended and the Fried Liver books can stay closed.} Bc5
( 4... Be7 5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 {Same structure: c3, d3, Re1. Your hands should know it by now.} )
( 4... h6 5. O-O d6 6. c3 {...h6 is slow. Castle and keep building.} )
( 4... d6 5. c3 Be7 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 )
( 4... d5 5. exd5 Nxd5 6. O-O Be7 7. Re1 {Black opened the center while their king is still in it. Re1 hits e5 straight away. [%cal Ge1e5,Gf3e5]} )
5. c3 {Transposes to the Pianissimo. Same position, different route. Chess is weird like that.} d6 6. O-O O-O 7. h3 a6 8. a4 Ba7 9. Re1

[Chapter "Black's sneaky 3rd moves"]
[Plans "Against the passive ...Be7 and ...d6 setups, build c3, d3, Re1 and then reroute Nbd2-f1-g3; Black is cramped, so d4 lands with extra force. After the Blackburne trap is dodged, play c3 to trade off that d4 pawn and take the center yourself. And if Black hands you e4 plus d4, keep both pawns side by side, develop Nc3, castle, and push only when it kicks something."]

{Not everyone plays 3...Bc5 or 3...Nf6. Here's how to handle the rest without falling for anything.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nd4 {The Blackburne Shilling trap. Black hopes you grab e5. [%cal Rf3e5]}
( 3... Be7 4. d3 Nf6 5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 {The Hungarian. Passive. Build your setup and they'll suffocate themselves.} )
( 3... d6 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 Be7 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 )
( 3... g6 4. c3 Bg7 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 {Black gave you the center for free. Say thank you.} )
4. Nxd4 {Trade it off. If you play 4.Nxe5?? then 4...Qg5! and things go very badly very fast.} exd4 5. O-O {Black's pawn on d4 is overextended and you're castled. Free advantage. [%cal Gc2c3] [%csl Rd4]}

[Chapter "When Black skips 2...Nc6"]
[Plans "Against the Philidor you have more space: Nc3, Be2, O-O, then f4 or Re1 and Bf1, and Black's pieces trip over each other. In the Petroff, Bd3, O-O, Re1 and c4 to undermine the e4 knight's support. After 2...f6, just develop fast and open lines toward the king; you're already winning, don't get cute."]

{Philidor, Petroff, and the cursed 2...f6. Each one has a simple answer.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6 {The Philidor: solid, passive, a bit sad.}
( 2... Nf6 {The Petroff. Black copies you again.} 3. Nxe5 {Take the pawn.} d6 {The correct move. Black kicks the knight first.}
  ( 3... Nxe4 {A classic blunder at your level. Copying moves fails here.} 4. Qe2 {If now 4...Nf6?? 5.Nc6+ is a discovered check that wins the queen. Look it up and enjoy it. [%cal Ge2e4,Ye5c6]} Qe7 5. Qxe4 d6 6. d4 {You're a pawn up. Keep the pin in mind and don't rush.} )
  4. Nf3 {Retreat. Don't get greedy.} Nxe4 5. d4 d5 6. Bd3 Be7 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 {The e4 knight feels the heat. Equal, but you have the easier plans.} )
( 2... f6 {The Damiano. Please punish this.} 3. Nxe5 fxe5
  ( 3... Qe7 {Black's best try. You still get a great position.} 4. Nf3 Qxe4+ 5. Be2 {Block, castle soon, enjoy a lead in development.} )
  4. Qh5+ {[%cal Gh5e8]} g6
  ( 4... Ke7 5. Qxe5+ Kf7 6. Bc4+ {The king is out for a walk. Bring everything and go mate it. [%cal Gc4f7]} )
  5. Qxe5+ Qe7 6. Qxh8 {A whole rook up. Look how open Black's king got.} )
3. d4 {Hit the center while Black is being shy. [%cal Gd4e5]} exd4
( 3... Nf6 4. Nc3 Nbd7 5. Bc4 Be7 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 {Classic Philidor setup. Keep the pressure on e5 and f7.} )
4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Be7 6. Be2 O-O 7. O-O {More space and easy development. Your job is to not lose it.}
